---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import ArticleLayout from '../../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import figComparatif from '../../../assets/img/fig-comparatif-soja.webp';
---

<ArticleLayout slug="raw-material-processing-soybean">
  <p>
    Raw soybean contains biologically active substances with an
    antinutritional effect. The most significant are essentially proteases
    and haemagglutinins, but saponins and factors inducing allergies, goitre
    and rickets are also present.
  </p>

  <h2>How does extrusion work?</h2>
  <p>
    Extrusion consists in passing soybeans, ground and compressed inside a
    barrel, through a die by means of a screw turning at high speed.
  </p>
  <h3>The aim of processing soybeans by extrusion is threefold</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>to reduce the content of antinutritional factors,</li>
    <li>not to damage the proteins,</li>
    <li>
      to rupture the cells containing the oil, in order to improve the energy
      value.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This method consists in bringing the soybeans to a high temperature —
    160&nbsp;°C at the die outlet — for a very short time. The internal
    configuration of the machine ensures adequate friction and shear
    conditions, which weaken the cell walls of the bean. The free water in the
    bean is brought to a temperature well above vaporisation point, but
    remains liquid because of the high pressures it withstands inside the body
    of the extruder. At the die outlet, the abrupt return to atmospheric
    pressure causes this water to evaporate instantly.
  </p>
  <h3>This evaporation has the effect of</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>bursting all the cells,</li>
    <li>
      absorbing the calories needed to produce this steam — around 6% of
      the initial weight of the beans.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The almost instantaneous return of the processed product to a temperature
    of around 100&nbsp;°C halts practically all browning reactions. This
    method is therefore akin to HT-ST (High Temperature–Short Time)
    treatments, somewhat similar to the UHT treatment used to produce
    long-life milk.
  </p>

  <h2>The energy content is higher</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>because its water content is low,</li>
    <li>
      because the process used to obtain it allows the practically complete
      destruction of the cell structures of the soybean, and thereby better
      digestion of the energy it contains.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The product is more stable</h2>
  <p>
    Extrusion also destroys the lipases and the lipoxidase contained in the
    raw soybean — these enzymes promote the hydrolysis and the oxidation of
    fats. Moreover, its reduced free water content after extrusion ensures its
    preservation: stored properly for months, the product
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>does not turn rancid,</li>
    <li>does not oxidise,</li>
    <li>does not go mouldy.</li>
  </ul>

